from common import lesson, lab, cite, out

LESSONS = [
lesson(13,4,'gradients-as-directions','Gradients as directions',
 'The gradient of a scalar function of a vector is itself a vector: the direction of steepest increase. For a dot product it is a constant, for a norm it is a unit vector, and that is most of the calculus this work needs.',
 '''<h2 id="derivative-of-a-dot">The derivative of a dot product</h2>
<p>Let f(v) = v · d for a fixed direction d. Change v by a small vector h and f changes by (v + h) · d − v · d = h · d, exactly, with no small-term approximation, because the dot product is linear. The <strong>gradient</strong> ∇f is the vector g such that the change in f for a small step h is g · h to first order, so for the dot product ∇f = d, a constant vector that does not depend on where you are. Coordinate by coordinate, ∂f/∂vᵢ = dᵢ, which you can read off from f = Σ vᵢ dᵢ.</p>
<div class="math">f(v) = v · d  ⇒  ∇f = d<br>f(v) = ‖v‖ = √(v · v)  ⇒  ∇f = v / ‖v‖  (undefined at v = 0)<br>f(v) = ‖v‖² = v · v  ⇒  ∇f = 2v<br>f(v) = v · d / ‖d‖  ⇒  ∇f = d̂   (the projection coefficient; gradient is the unit direction)</div>
<p>For the squared norm, f = Σ vᵢ², so ∂f/∂vᵢ = 2vᵢ and ∇f = 2v. For the norm itself apply the chain rule to the square root: ∇‖v‖ = (1 / (2‖v‖)) · 2v = v/‖v‖, the unit vector pointing along v. The norm grows fastest if you keep going the way you were going, at rate one. That is the entire derivation, and it is the only chain-rule computation in the course.</p>
<h2 id="meaning">Gradient as direction</h2>
<p>A gradient is a vector in the same space as v, and it answers a geometric question: of all unit directions u, which makes f increase fastest for a tiny step? The answer is u = ∇f/‖∇f‖, and the rate of increase in direction u is ∇f · u, a projection coefficient (lesson 03). Stepping along the gradient is <strong>gradient ascent</strong>; stepping against it is gradient descent, the procedure that trained every weight in the fixture. For f(v) = v · d̂ the gradient is d̂, a step of length s along it increases the projection coefficient by exactly s, and a step of the same length in a direction orthogonal to d̂ changes it by zero.</p>
<p>The lab checks the two analytic gradients against central finite differences, (f(v + hεᵢ) − f(v − hεᵢ)) / 2h, on the first five coordinates of a real activation, then takes one ascent step.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py gradient</code></pre>
'''+out('''token 12 '42'; f(v) = v.d with d the digit direction
analytic gradient of v.d is d; first five entries [0.753 0.62  2.768 0.615 1.665]
numeric gradient (central differences) on those five coordinates [0.753 0.62  2.768 0.615 1.665]
analytic gradient of |v| is v/|v|; first five [-0.025 -0.029  0.016  0.019 -0.022]
numeric on those five coordinates [-0.025 -0.029  0.016  0.019 -0.022]
gradient ascent on f(v) = v.d/|d|: coefficient 19.71 -> 24.71 after a step of length 5.0 (predicted increase exactly 5.0)
same step length in a random direction: 19.71 -> 19.52
norm after the step along d: 88.51 -> 89.75; cos(x, d) = 0.223''')+'''
<p>The numeric and analytic gradients agree to the printed precision in both cases. The ascent step is the formula box made concrete: a step of length 5 along d̂ raised the projection coefficient from 19.71 to 24.71, exactly 5, while a step of length 5 in a random direction moved it by −0.19, the random direction’s small chance component along d̂. The norm grew only from 88.51 to 89.75, because the step was mostly orthogonal to x (their cosine is 0.223); by Pythagoras, ‖x + 5d̂‖² = ‖x‖² + 2·5·(x · d̂) + 25 = 7,833.8 + 197.1 + 25 = 8,055.9, and √8,055.9 = 89.75.</p>
<h2 id="where-it-goes">Where this is used</h2>
<p>Three places, all the same computation. <em>Attribution:</em> the gradient of a scalar output, such as a logit or a probe score, with respect to an activation is a vector in activation space; its dot product with a change in the activation predicts, to first order, the change in the output, so gradient-times-activation is a first-order estimate of each component’s contribution. <em>Feature visualisation and steering:</em> gradient ascent on an input or an activation to maximise a scalar is repeated steps along ∇f, and the lesson’s step is one iteration of it with f linear. <em>Training:</em> every weight in W_Q was set by gradient descent on a loss, which is why the fixture exists. In a framework such as PyTorch the gradient is computed for you by backpropagation; this lesson is so that you can check one by hand and know what shape it must have: the gradient of a scalar with respect to a (768,) activation is a (768,) vector, with respect to a (768, 64) matrix is a (768, 64) matrix.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Gradient-based attribution assumes the first-order approximation is good over the size of change considered. For a linear function such as a projection it is exact, as the output shows; for a model with nonlinearities it is exact only for infinitesimal steps, and the check is to take the actual step and compare. The <a href="../../../interpretability/interpretability-tut/index.html">interpretability course</a> makes that comparison on real attribution methods.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Reading the gradient of the norm as “the direction the model wants to grow”. ∇‖v‖ = v/‖v‖ is a fact about the norm function and holds for any vector whatsoever; it carries no information about the model. A gradient is interesting when the function is, and the dot product with a fixed direction is interesting only when the direction is.</p></div>''',
 'f(v) = (v · d̂)² is the squared projection coefficient. What is its gradient at a real activation x, and in which direction does it point?',
 'By the chain rule on g(v) = v · d̂ with ∇g = d̂, ∇f = 2 (v · d̂) d̂ = 2c d̂, where c is the current coefficient. It points along d̂ when c is positive and against d̂ when c is negative: squared projection grows by moving further from the hyperplane c = 0 in whichever direction the token is already on. Its magnitude 2|c| is zero on that hyperplane, where the function has its minimum.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py gradient --token 147'],
 'Token 147 (<code>&#39; a&#39;</code>) was the least digit-like token in lesson 02. Take the same ascent step from it and verify the predicted increase of 5.0.',
 'The coefficient rises by exactly 5.0 again, from wherever it started, because the gradient of a linear function is the same everywhere. The starting coefficient will be negative. Whether adding 5d̂ to the activation of “a” would make the model behave as if it saw a digit is a question about the model, not about this arithmetic, and only an intervention experiment answers it.'), ['3b1b','arena']),

lesson(14,4,'is-the-correlation-real','Is this correlation real?',
 'A direction’s projection correlates with a label. Before believing it, see what shuffled labels and random directions produce on tokens the direction never saw.',
 '''<h2 id="correlation">Correlation is a cosine</h2>
<p>The <strong>correlation</strong> between two sequences of n numbers a and b is the cosine similarity of their centred versions: subtract each mean, then divide the dot product by the two norms. It lies between −1 and 1, is unchanged by scaling or shifting either sequence, and is zero when the centred sequences are orthogonal. Everything you learned about cosines in lesson 02 applies: two sequences that are both dominated by a shared component correlate highly for that reason alone, and a correlation of 0.4 means nothing until you know what unrelated sequences of this kind produce.</p>
<div class="math">corr(a, b) = (a − ā) · (b − b̄) / (‖a − ā‖ ‖b − b̄‖)</div>
<p>In this course a is the projection of each token onto a direction and b is a label. The lesson-09 probe reached correlation 0.986 with the digit label on held-out tokens. Three questions decide whether that is a finding.</p>
<h2 id="three-questions">Three questions, three baselines</h2>
<p><em>Was the direction fitted on these tokens?</em> If so, the correlation is a training score and lesson 09 showed it is 1.000 for any label whatsoever. Only <strong>held-out</strong> tokens count. <em>What correlation would shuffled labels produce?</em> Permuting the label among the held-out tokens destroys any relation while preserving the label’s proportions and the projection’s distribution; the spread of correlations over many shuffles is the <strong>permutation</strong> null, and an observed value well outside it is unlikely to be chance. <em>What would a random direction produce?</em> Projecting the held-out tokens onto directions drawn at random tells you how much correlation with this label the geometry of the activations hands out for free, which is a different question from the permutation one and, on this fixture, has a very different answer.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py correlation --label is_digit</code></pre>
'''+out('''label is_digit; direction fitted on 211 tokens; correlation train 1.000, held out 0.986
permutation baseline on the held-out tokens (200 shuffles of the label): mean 0.002, 97.5th percentile 0.219, max 0.364
shuffles with |r| >= observed held-out |r|: 0 of 200
random-direction baseline (held out, 200 directions): mean 0.008, max |r| 0.620
the same projection correlated with other labels on held-out tokens: after_period 0.000, starts_with_space -0.022, capitalised -0.067''')+'''
<p>The permutation null for 90 held-out tokens with 13 or so digits among them has mean 0.002 and a 97.5th percentile of 0.219; its largest value in 200 shuffles was 0.364. The observed 0.986 is outside anything the shuffles produced, so the relation between this direction’s projection and the label is not an accident of these 90 tokens. The random-direction baseline tells a different story: of 200 random directions, the best reached a held-out correlation of 0.620 with the digit label. Digit tokens form a tight cluster (lesson 02 found cosines above 0.7 among them), and any direction with a chance component along the cluster’s offset from the rest correlates with the label somewhat. So “a direction correlates 0.6 with is_digit” would not be a finding on this fixture, and “0.986” is a finding mainly because it is far above 0.620. Which baseline you need depends on the claim: shuffled labels test “is there a relation at all”, random directions test “is this particular direction better than nothing”.</p>
<p>The last line asks whether the direction is specifically about digits or about something correlated with digits in this passage. Its projection has correlation 0.000 with after_period, −0.022 with starts_with_space and −0.067 with capitalised on the held-out tokens. That rules out three confounds; it does not rule out the ones you did not list, and in a passage where every number followed the word “counted”, a direction reading “follows counted” would pass every test here.</p>
<h2 id="what-this-is-not">What this is and is not</h2>
<p>This lesson is the geometric minimum: a held-out split, a permutation null, a random-direction null, and a look at alternative labels. It does not give you confidence intervals on the correlation, a correction for having tried several directions or labels before reporting one, or a sample-size calculation for how many held-out tokens you need to tell 0.6 from 0.98. Those are statistics, and the <a href="../../../statistics/statistics-tut/index.html">statistics course</a> has a lesson on correlation, permutation tests and multiple-comparison control for screening many candidate directions; take this lesson as the question and that one as the method. The rigor framing of the roadmap applies here exactly: write down the held-out correlation that would count as success, and the baseline that would also pass if the claim were empty, before you look.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>“Feature 1234 fires on digits” is a correlation claim between a projection and a label. The questions are the same: which tokens were held out, what do shuffled labels give, what does a random direction give on this data, and what else does the projection correlate with. A claim that cannot report the first and either of the next two has not been tested.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Using the permutation null alone when the label marks a tight cluster. It will say almost any direction is “significant”, because almost any direction separates a cluster a little, and 0.3 is far outside the shuffle distribution. The random-direction baseline is the one that calibrates “how good is this direction”.</p></div>''',
 'The permutation 97.5th percentile is 0.219 and the random-direction maximum is 0.620. A candidate direction scores 0.45 on held-out tokens. Is it a digit direction?',
 'It passes the permutation test, so its projection is related to the label beyond chance. It fails the random-direction comparison: random directions reach 0.62 on this fixture, so 0.45 is worse than what geometry gives away for free, and the direction has no demonstrated advantage over noise as a digit detector. The honest report is “related to digit tokens, but no better than a random direction on this layer and sample”. If the direction came from an external hypothesis, that is still informative; if it was selected from many candidates, it is not even that.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py correlation --label after_period --perms 100'],
 'Run the baselines for the after-period label with 100 shuffles. Compare the held-out correlation with the two nulls.',
 'The held-out correlation is lower than for digits (lesson 09 found about 0.73 to 0.78 depending on ridge) and the random-direction maximum is lower too, because sentence-initial tokens do not cluster as tightly as numbers. Judge the direction by the gap between its score and the random-direction maximum, not by the permutation percentile alone.'), ['arena','3b1b']),

lesson(15,4,'readiness-review','Readiness review',
 'Given a cached activation matrix and a label, find a direction, test it on tokens you did not use, run PCA and SVD, and say what each number means. The rubric is what a reviewer would ask.',
 '''<h2 id="task">The task</h2>
<p>You are handed an activation matrix X of shape (n, d), a label y of length n, and the weight matrices of one component. In one sitting, without the lessons open, you should be able to do the following and narrate it. (1) Report shapes and norms, and decide which rows to exclude and why. (2) Split the tokens, fit a least-squares (or ridge) direction on one part, and report its held-out correlation. (3) Report a permutation baseline and a random-direction baseline for that correlation. (4) Run PCA on the training rows, report variance explained for the first few components, and state the cosine between your direction and the top component. (5) Remove your direction from the activations, refit, and report what remains readable. (6) Run the SVD of a weight product, report its rank and the shape of its singular value spectrum. (7) For every number, say in one sentence what it measures and what it does not establish. The lab’s <code>review</code> command runs steps 2 to 5 end to end on the fixture.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py review --label is_digit</code></pre>
'''+out('''readiness run on is_digit: 211 train, 90 held out
1 direction by least squares: |w| 0.0374; held-out correlation 0.986
2 permutation baseline: 97.5th percentile 0.219
3 PCA on train: variance explained [0.075 0.061 0.055 0.041 0.036]; correlation of each component score with is_digit on held out: 0.191 0.517 -0.145 0.738 -0.124
4 cos(w, PCA component 1) = 0.055
5 after removing w: refit held-out correlation 0.696''')+'''
<h2 id="narration">A model narration</h2>
<p>“The least-squares direction w, fitted on 211 of the 301 non-outlier tokens, predicts the digit label on the other 90 with correlation 0.986. Shuffling the label gives at most about 0.22 at the 97.5th percentile, and lesson 14’s random directions reached 0.62, so the direction is both related to the label and far better than chance geometry. PCA on the training rows has a slow scree (7.5%, 6.1%, 5.5%, …), and no single component is the digit direction: the best, component 4, correlates 0.738 with the label on held-out tokens, and w has cosine 0.055 with component 1, so the probe is nearly orthogonal to the dominant variation, which lesson 11 found to track position. Removing w from every token and refitting leaves a held-out correlation of 0.696: the label remains partly linearly readable from the remaining 767 dimensions, so w is a direction that reads digit-ness, not the only one, and the claim ‘the digit information lives along w’ is not supported. Nothing here says what the model does with w.”</p>
<p>Notice what the narration does at each step: it names the sample the number came from, the baseline it was compared with, and the stronger claim it declines to make. That is the competence the course is after.</p>
<h2 id="rubric">Rubric</h2>
<table class="rubric"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Ready</th><th>Not yet</th></tr>
<tr><td>Shapes</td><td>Writes (n, d), (d, h), (n,) for every object before computing, and predicts the shape of each product.</td><td>Finds out shapes by running the code and reading errors.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Projection</td><td>States whether the direction was normalised and reports x · d̂ per token; checks residual ⟂ d.</td><td>Reports x · d for directions of different norms and compares them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Norms and outliers</td><td>Computes norms first, names excluded rows, and says how results change with them.</td><td>Runs PCA on the raw matrix and reports an 89% first component.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Basis</td><td>Explains why a direction need not be a neuron and what a rotation of the stream would change.</td><td>Reads coordinate 447 as “neuron 447 does X”.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rank</td><td>Predicts rank 64 for a product through a 64-dimensional space and checks it in float64.</td><td>Reports the numerical rank of a float32 product as 768.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Least squares</td><td>Splits first; reports held-out correlation; knows the training score of an overparameterised probe is 1.</td><td>Reports the training correlation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baselines</td><td>Reports a permutation null and a random-direction null and says which claim each tests.</td><td>Reports one correlation and the word “significant”.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Removal</td><td>Refits after removal and reports what remains readable on held-out tokens.</td><td>Concludes a feature is erased because the removed direction now reads zero.</td></tr>
<tr><td>PCA</td><td>Reports variance explained, describes the scree, and separates “direction of variance” from “direction of the label”.</td><td>Reads the first component as the model’s most important feature.</td></tr>
<tr><td>SVD</td><td>Reads singular values as stretch factors, right vectors as inputs read, left vectors as outputs written, in the stated convention.</td><td>Confuses U and V, or reads a flat spectrum as rank one.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gradients</td><td>Writes ∇(v · d) = d and ∇‖v‖ = v/‖v‖ and checks one against finite differences.</td><td>Needs a framework to differentiate a dot product.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claims about models</td><td>Every statement about what GPT-2 “does” is either geometry about the fixture or cited.</td><td>Generalises from one passage to “GPT-2 encodes position”.</td></tr></table>
<h2 id="held-out-families">What a tutor may ask instead</h2>
<p>The fixture is public, so a tutor assessing readiness will vary it. The families of variants, described but not published, per the assessment contract: a different passage through the same layer; a different layer of the same passage; a label the fixture does not include, computed from the tokens; a different attention head’s weights, possibly one with a steep singular spectrum; an activation matrix with the outlier row left in without warning; a label that is a confound of another (every digit preceded by the same word); and a direction supplied by the tutor rather than fitted, to be tested rather than found. The commands in this course answer all of them with no new code, which is the point.</p>
<h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
<p>The <a href="../../../transformers/transformers-tut/index.html">transformers course</a> puts W_Q, W_K, W_V and W_O back inside attention, with layer norm, biases, masking and softmax, and derives why QK and OV products are the objects to study. The <a href="../../../interpretability/interpretability-tut/index.html">interpretability course</a> replaces the digit label with a neuron’s own activations and a claimed explanation, and asks for the prediction test before the look. The <a href="../appendices/d-reading-map.html">reading map</a> lists the 3Blue1Brown chapters that supply the geometric arguments this course only stated, and the ARENA exercises that make you implement all of this in PyTorch on a live model.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Every item in the rubric is a question you will be asked, or should ask, about a published direction. The ones most often missing from write-ups are the random-direction baseline and the post-removal refit.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Treating a passed rubric as a finding about GPT-2. The fixture is 301 tokens of one passage at one layer; it demonstrates the moves, not any property of the model. The findings come from the next two courses, with more data and a prediction written down first.</p></div>''',
 'Step 5 of the review removes w and the refitted probe still reaches 0.696, while lesson 08 removed the mean-difference direction and the refit fell to −0.053. Both directions read the digit label well before removal. Why can two good directions behave so differently under removal?',
 'Because “reads the label” and “carries the label’s linearly readable variance” are different properties. The mean-difference direction points along the offset between the class means; removing it collapses the means together, and on this fixture little readable signal survives in other directions. The least-squares w, with 768 parameters and 211 rows, is one of infinitely many exact fits and is partly shaped by noise directions of small variance; removing it takes out that particular combination, and the class offset remains largely intact along other directions, which a refit finds. Removal tests a specific direction; it does not test a concept, and the two outcomes are consistent with each other.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py review --label after_period'],
 'Run the review on the after-period label and write the narration yourself, one sentence per numbered line, each ending with what the number does not establish.',
 'Your held-out correlation will be around 0.7, the permutation percentile similar to the digit case, no component will dominate, and the post-removal refit will tell you whether sentence-initial information is spread across directions on this fixture. The narration is the deliverable; the numbers are its evidence.'), ['framework','toy','arena']),
]
